Conformance fixes
=================

Found by tests/test_processor_tests.c, which runs SingleStepTests against the
core. Each changes emulated behaviour, so code that depended on the old
behaviour will see a difference.

(1)		P bit 4		Was forced set on every instruction for the 65C02, which
					applies the 65C816's emulation-mode rule to the wrong CPU.
					Bit 4 has no register bit behind it on a 65C02 and is now
					preserved. PLP and RTI no longer load it from the stack.

(2)		$5C $DC $FC	Were one-byte implied NOPs, so their operand bytes were
					executed. Now three bytes, as on a real 65C02. $5C is 8
					cycles per the WDC datasheet and the 6502.org opcode list;
					SingleStepTests says 4, and that divergence is recorded in
					the baseline rather than matched.

(3)		SBC			Decimal mode used the NMOS per-nibble correction. The
					65C02 subtracts in binary and corrects afterwards. The two
					differ only when a digit is $A-$F. Overflow was also
					computed from the carry the instruction had just written
					rather than the one it started with.

(4)		BRK			Cost 14 cycles: interrupt6502() charges seven for the
					interrupt sequence, which BRK also pays through the opcode
					table. IRQ and NMI still need it, as neither goes through
					the table.

(5)		Timing		JMP (abs) is 6 cycles, not the NMOS 5. ASL, ROL, LSR and
					ROR abs,X are 6 plus one only when indexing crosses a page,
					where the NMOS always paid it. BIT abs,X gains the same
					conditional cycle. The variant opcode files may now override
					6502.opcodes rather than only adding to it.

(6)		65C816		A 16-bit operand costs an extra cycle, which only LDA
					charged. PHX, PHY, PLX and PLY charged the accumulator
					width instead of the index width, and TSB charged nothing.

(7)		65C816		(dp), (dp,X), (dp),Y and (sr),Y ignored the data bank and
					used bank 0. Indexing now carries past $FFFF into the next
					bank.

The read-modify-write double write noted above is still not emulated, and shows
up as a bus-trace divergence in the same tests.






